---
import Layout from "../layouts/Layout.astro";
import WorkDossier from "../components/WorkDossier.astro";
import { projects } from "../data/projects";
import { Image } from "astro:assets";
import avatar from "../assets/avatar.png";
---

<Layout
  title="Portfolio | Practical Agent Workflows"
  description="A single-page portfolio focused on practical agent workflows, long-running automation systems, and careful execution."
  lang="en"
>
  <main class="page-shell">
    <div class="lang-switch-row">
      <a class="lang-switch" href="/zh/" title="切换到中文">
        <span class="lang-switch-current">EN</span>
        <span class="lang-switch-divider" aria-hidden="true"></span>
        <span class="lang-switch-target">中</span>
      </a>
    </div>
    <section class="hero" aria-labelledby="hero-heading">
      <p class="hero-kicker">Hi, I am Zhou Le.</p>
      <h1 id="hero-heading">I build agent workflows that already run outside the demo.</h1>
      <p class="hero-lead">
        This site is a small record of systems I have built while learning AI tooling, coding
        agents, and workflow automation through practice.
      </p>
      <p class="hero-supporting">
        I am still early in this work, but I care most about systems that keep their state,
        recover context, and stay useful after the first successful run.
      </p>
      <p class="status-line">
        <span class="status-dot" aria-hidden="true"></span>
        A small record of real work, not a personal brand exercise.
      </p>
    </section>

    <section class="about-bridge" aria-labelledby="about-heading">
      <div class="about-bridge-grid">
        <div class="profile-card">
          <div class="profile-card-frame">
            <Image
              class="profile-card-avatar"
              src={avatar}
              alt="Portrait of Zhou Le"
              width={144}
              height={144}
              loading="lazy"
            />
          </div>
          <div class="profile-card-copy">
            <p class="profile-card-name">Zhou Le</p>
            <dl class="profile-card-facts">
              <div class="profile-card-fact">
                <dt>Focus</dt>
                <dd>Agent workflows</dd>
              </div>
              <div class="profile-card-fact">
                <dt>Background</dt>
                <dd>B.Eng. in Software Engineering; M.A. in Translation</dd>
              </div>
              <div class="profile-card-fact">
                <dt>Tools</dt>
                <dd>Codex, Opencode, OpenClaw</dd>
              </div>
              <div class="profile-card-fact">
                <dt>GitHub</dt>
                <dd>
                  <a
                    class="profile-card-link"
                    href="https://github.com/fancydirty"
                    target="_blank"
                    rel="noreferrer"
                  >
                    fancydirty
                  </a>
                </dd>
              </div>
              <div class="profile-card-fact">
                <dt>Social</dt>
                <dd>
                  <a
                    class="profile-card-link"
                    href="https://www.linkedin.com/in/%E4%B9%90-%E5%91%A8-967660354/"
                    target="_blank"
                    rel="noreferrer"
                  >
                    LinkedIn
                  </a>
                  <span class="profile-card-sep">·</span>
                  <a
                    class="profile-card-link"
                    href="https://space.bilibili.com/5582337?spm_id_from=333.1007.0.0"
                    target="_blank"
                    rel="noreferrer"
                  >
                    Bilibili <span class="social-badge">380k</span>
                  </a>
                </dd>
              </div>
            </dl>
          </div>
        </div>

        <div class="about-bridge-copy">
        <p class="eyebrow">About Me</p>
        <h2 id="about-heading">I came to systems work through language, and I am learning it by building.</h2>
        <p>
          I hold a master's degree in translation from UESTC. Before I started building agent
          workflows, I spent years working with language and content. That background made me care
          about precision, structure, and whether something still makes sense once another person
          has to pick it up.
        </p>
        <p>
          Over the last year, I have turned that same habit toward AI tooling, coding agents, and
          workflow automation. I learn by building systems that need to keep running, seeing where
          they drift, and then tightening the state, checks, and guardrails until they behave
          better. Codex, Opencode, and OpenClaw are the tools I return to most. The projects below
          are the clearest record of that shift so far.
        </p>
        </div>
      </div>
    </section>

    <WorkDossier projects={projects} />

    <section class="lower-grid">
        <section class="manifesto" aria-labelledby="principles-heading">
        <p class="eyebrow">Operating Principles</p>
        <h2 id="principles-heading">What the work has taught me so far.</h2>
        <p class="principles-lead">
          I used to think the hard part of agent workflows was writing better prompts. After three systems, I realized the hard part is designing for failures you cannot fully predict. These are the patterns that keep showing up.
        </p>
        <ul class="principles-list">
          <li><strong>"Almost working" is the most expensive state.</strong> A system that fails loudly is cheap to fix. One that fails quietly — a partial season that looks complete, a transferred file in the wrong directory — becomes expensive fast. I design for fast, visible failure.</li>
          <li><strong>State should outlive the session.</strong> If an agent cannot pick up where it left off after a crash, the state was in the wrong place. This has held true across every project, from content pipelines to media tracking to long-running internal ops.</li>
          <li><strong>Explicit boundaries beat smart loops.</strong> A single clever loop feels elegant until it meets an edge case. I'd rather have three dumb contracts that each do one thing well than one loop that tries to be smart about everything.</li>
          <li><strong>The system must be able to refuse.</strong> A workflow that always produces <em>something</em> will eventually produce garbage. I build in explicit "I can't do this yet" states, because honest failure costs less than silent success.</li>
        </ul>
      </section>

      <section class="now-panel" aria-labelledby="now-heading">
        <p class="eyebrow">Now</p>
        <h2 id="now-heading">Rebuilding in public, one step at a time.</h2>
        <p>
          Right now I'm trying to solve a tension: agent skills need to be opinionated enough to actually work, but configurable enough to survive on someone else's machine.
        </p>
        <p>
          Most open-source agent tools fall on one side or the other — either too rigid to adapt, or so flexible that they become prompts with extra steps. I don't have a clean answer yet. The next thing I build will be an experiment in finding that balance.
        </p>

        <ul class="links-list" aria-label="links">
          <li>
            <a href="mailto:fancydirty@gmail.com">fancydirty@gmail.com</a>
          </li>
        </ul>
      </section>
    </section>

    <footer class="site-footer">
      <p class="footer-social">
        Also on
        <a href="https://github.com/fancydirty" target="_blank" rel="noreferrer">GitHub</a>
        ·
        <a href="https://www.linkedin.com/in/%E4%B9%90-%E5%91%A8-967660354/" target="_blank" rel="noreferrer">LinkedIn</a>
        ·
        <a href="https://space.bilibili.com/5582337?spm_id_from=333.1007.0.0" target="_blank" rel="noreferrer">Bilibili</a>
      </p>
      <p>
        This site is intentionally small. The goal is to show real systems, modestly and
        clearly.
      </p>
    </footer>
  </main>
</Layout>
